      "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2312.03687v1",
      "title": "MatterGen: a generative model for inorganic materials design",
      "publish_date": "2023-12-07",
      "excerpts": [
        "A crystalline material can be defined by its repeating unit, i.e., its unit cell, which encodes the atom types A (i.e., chemical elements), coordinates X , and periodic lattice L (Appendices A.1 and A.2). We define a corruption process for each component that suits their own geometry and has physically motivated limiting noise distributions.\nof generated structures\nMatterGen improves upon limitations of previous methods [4, 49] by intro- ducing a joint diffusion process for atom types, coordinates, and lattice (Section 2.1 and Appendices A.5 to A.7), which—combined with the substantially larger training dataset Alex-MP-20—drastically increase the stability, uniqueness, and\nof generated structures\nn ∈ N Number of atoms in a crystal M = ( X , A , L ) A crystal structure X ∈ [0 , 1) 3 × n Fractional atomic coordinates ˜ X ∈ R 3 × n Cartesian atomic coordinates A ∈ A n Atomic species in a crystal L = ( l 1 , l 2 , l 3 ) ∈ R 3 × 3 The unit cell lattice matrix l j ∈ R 3 , j ∈ { 1 , 2 , 3 } The j -th lattice\nof generated structures\nAny crystal structure can be represented by some repeating unit (called the unit cell ) that tiles the entire 3D space. The unit cell itself contains a number of atoms that are arranged inside of it. Thus, we use the following universal representation for a material M : M = ( A , X , L ) , (A1)\nof generated structures\nwhere A = ( a 1 , a 2 , . . . , a n ) ⊤ ∈ A n are the atomic species of the atoms inside the unit cell; L = ( l 1 , l 2 , l 3 ) ∈ R 3 × 3 is the lattice, i.e., the shape of the repeating unit cell; and X = ( x 1 , x 2 , . . . , x n ) ∈ [0 , 1) 3 × n are the fractional coordinates of the atoms inside the unit cell.\nof generated structures\nTo understand the choice of the mean in the limit distribution, recall that the volume of a parallelepiped L is | det L | . We introduce a scalar coefficient µ ( n ) that depends on the number of atoms in the cell to make the atomic density of the mean noisy lattice roughly constant for differently sized systems.\nof generated structures\nFor layer l , we denote the edge representation of the edge ( ij k ) ∈ E between atoms i and j as m l ij k ∈ R d , where i is inside the unit cell and j is k ∈ Z 3 unit cells displaced from the center unit cell. We use a multi-layer perceptron (MLP) ϕ l : R d → R to predict a scalar score per edge.\nof generated structures\nThe Alex-MP-ICSD dataset is used as a reference for the computation of stability (i.e., energy above hull) and uniqueness of generated structures. To train MatterGen, we employ a subset of the Alex-MP-ICSD dataset, selecting only structures with up to 20 atoms and whose energy above hull\n31\nof generated structures > Og\nFor every chemical system, we performed two rounds of RSS using the airss [45] pack- age. In each round, we generated 300,000 structures by sampling 100,000 structures across three different ranges of number of atoms per unit cell."
